const ARTICLES = [
{id:"blog-1",cat:"Bookkeeping",date:"Aug 12, 2026",read:6,title:"Cash vs. Accrual: When to Switch & Why It Matters at Scale",sub:"A practical guide for growing businesses",tags:["Cash basis","Accrual","IRS","Deferred revenue"],body:`<p>Cash-basis accounting works beautifully, right up until the moment it doesn't. When you're small, recording money as it moves in and out of your bank account is simple and perfectly adequate. But as a business scales, cash accounting starts quietly hiding the truth about performance, and the gap between what your bank balance says and what your business actually earned grows every month.</p><h4>The three signals that force a switch</h4><p>Three signals reliably indicate it's time to move to accrual accounting. First, you carry meaningful inventory: cash accounting cannot properly match the cost of goods to the revenue they generate. Second, you invoice on terms (net-30, net-60), so revenue earned and cash received drift apart. Third, you cross a regulatory threshold that mandates accrual reporting.</p><ul><li>US: the IRS generally requires accrual once average annual gross receipts exceed the inflation-adjusted threshold.</li><li>Inventory-heavy businesses are effectively pushed to accrual regardless of size.</li><li>Investors, lenders and acquirers almost always expect accrual-basis statements.</li></ul><h4>How to convert cleanly</h4><p>A cash-to-accrual conversion requires restating opening balances: booking receivables for revenue earned but not collected, payables for expenses incurred but not paid, and deferring revenue collected in advance. Pick a conversion date that aligns with a period end, build a reconciliation bridge and run one comparative period in parallel.</p><blockquote>The single biggest mistake we see is switching mid-year without restating the opening balance sheet. That produces a distorted P&amp;L that satisfies nobody.</blockquote><div class="callout"><b>Rule of thumb:</b> if you're raising capital, carrying inventory or invoicing on terms, you've likely outgrown cash accounting already.</div><h4>Handling deferred revenue in the switch</h4><p>Any cash collected before delivering the product or service is a liability, not income, under accrual. Build a deferred-revenue schedule for every open contract, recognise the earned portion and carry the rest forward before you close the first accrual period.</p>`},
{id:"blog-4",cat:"Bookkeeping",date:"Aug 20, 2026",read:7,title:"Month-End Close in 5 Days: A Repeatable Checklist",sub:"Turn a chaotic scramble into a predictable process",tags:["Month-end close","Checklist","Reconciliation"],body:`<p>A slow, unpredictable month-end close is one of the clearest signs that a finance function is running on heroics rather than systems. Teams that close in fifteen days aren't working harder than those that close in five; they're working without a checklist, cut-offs or clear ownership.</p><h4>Day 1–2: Cut-off and capture</h4><p>Freeze the sub-ledgers. Make sure every bill, invoice and expense report for the period is in the system. Post recurring journals and reconcile cash first: the bank is your anchor of truth.</p><ul><li>Lock AP and AR at the agreed cut-off</li><li>Reconcile all bank and credit-card accounts</li><li>Post recurring journals (rent, depreciation, prepaids)</li><li>Accrue for goods and services received but not yet invoiced</li></ul><h4>Day 3: Reconcile everything that isn't cash</h4><p>Reconcile the balance-sheet accounts that hide errors: prepaids, accruals, fixed assets, intercompany, payroll clearing and sales-tax control accounts.</p><h4>Day 4: Review and flux</h4><p>Run a variance analysis, month over month and actual vs. budget. Anything above your materiality threshold gets an explanation before the books close.</p><blockquote>If leadership is discovering variances in the board meeting, your close is a day too late.</blockquote><h4>Day 5: Report and sign off</h4><p>Produce the management pack with a one-page commentary, sign off, archive the workpapers and update the checklist with anything that slowed you down.</p><div class="callout"><b>The compounding win:</b> a five-day close means decisions are made on fresh data, and your team gets weeks back for analysis instead of assembly.</div>`},
{id:"blog-5",cat:"Bookkeeping",date:"Jul 30, 2026",read:5,title:"Bank Reconciliation: The Discipline That Catches Everything",sub:"Why the humble rec is your best fraud and error control",tags:["Bank reconciliation","Controls","Fraud prevention"],body:`<p>Bank reconciliation is the least glamorous task in accounting and the most valuable control you have. Every duplicated payment, missed deposit, bank fee and fraudulent transaction eventually surfaces in a reconciliation, if you actually do one.</p><h4>What a real reconciliation checks</h4><p>A proper rec doesn't just confirm the ending balance matches. It investigates every reconciling item, including anything on the statement that isn't in your books.</p><ul><li>Match every cleared transaction to a booked entry</li><li>Investigate stale outstanding cheques (over 90 days)</li><li>Flag any bank-side item with no book entry</li><li>Confirm transfers appear on both sides</li></ul><blockquote>The transaction you didn't record is far more dangerous than the one you recorded twice.</blockquote><h4>Reconcile weekly at scale</h4><p>For high-volume businesses, monthly reconciliation lets errors compound for weeks. A weekly cadence surfaces problems while they're cheap to fix.</p><div class="callout"><b>Automation helps, judgement decides:</b> bank feeds speed up matching, but a person still needs to review exceptions.</div>`},
{id:"blog-2",cat:"Tax",date:"Aug 05, 2026",read:9,title:"US Multi-State Nexus in 2026: A CPA's Field Guide",sub:"Economic nexus, marketplace rules and what changed",tags:["Sales tax","Nexus","Wayfair","VDA"],body:`<p>Since the Supreme Court's Wayfair decision, US sales tax has become a minefield for any business selling across state lines. States continue to adjust economic-nexus thresholds and marketplace-facilitator rules, and every change alters which clients have a filing obligation.</p><h4>Economic vs. physical nexus</h4><p>Physical nexus is the old rule: an office, employee, inventory or property in a state creates an obligation. Economic nexus is the Wayfair rule: cross a dollar or transaction threshold and you owe sales tax there even with no physical presence.</p><ul><li>Many states use a US$100,000 sales threshold, some with a transaction count</li><li>Several states have dropped transaction-count thresholds</li><li>Measurement periods differ by state</li><li>Marketplace sales may or may not count toward your threshold</li></ul><h4>The marketplace-facilitator shift</h4><p>Marketplaces now collect and remit on behalf of sellers in most states, but that doesn't eliminate registration obligations everywhere, and direct website sales still count.</p><blockquote>A seller can be fully compliant on Amazon and exposed on their own store in the same state.</blockquote><h4>Evaluating exposure</h4><p>Map physical presence, pull sales by state for the trailing twelve months, compare against each threshold and separate marketplace from direct sales. Where there's historical exposure, consider a Voluntary Disclosure Agreement.</p><div class="callout"><b>Watch-list:</b> re-run every client's nexus review at least annually.</div>`},
{id:"blog-6",cat:"Tax",date:"Aug 18, 2026",read:8,title:"1099 vs. W-2: Getting Worker Classification Right",sub:"The misclassification mistake that triggers audits",tags:["1099","W-2","Worker classification","ABC test"],body:`<p>Worker classification is one of the most audited areas in US payroll and tax. Getting it wrong exposes a business to back taxes, penalties and interest that can dwarf the savings that motivated it.</p><h4>The core tests</h4><p>The IRS looks at behavioural control, financial control and the relationship of the parties. Some states apply stricter standards; California's ABC test presumes employment unless all three prongs are proven.</p><ul><li>Behavioural: who controls how, when and where the work is done?</li><li>Financial: who bears the risk of profit or loss and provides tools?</li><li>Relationship: is there a written contract, benefits and permanence?</li></ul><blockquote>If you control the how, not just the what, you almost certainly have an employee.</blockquote><h4>Cross-border complications</h4><p>For contractors abroad, classification interacts with permanent-establishment risk and local labour law.</p><div class="callout"><b>Guardrail:</b> full-time hours, exclusively for you, on your systems and schedule is a red flag. Get advice before the relationship deepens.</div>`},
{id:"blog-7",cat:"Tax",date:"Jul 22, 2026",read:7,title:"R&D Tax Credits: Money Most Companies Leave on the Table",sub:"Innovation incentives in the US and Canada",tags:["R&D credit","SR&ED","Innovation"],body:`<p>R&amp;D tax incentives are among the most generous and most underclaimed reliefs available. Much everyday software, engineering and process-improvement work qualifies.</p><h4>What actually qualifies</h4><p>The common thread is technological uncertainty resolved through systematic experimentation: a novel integration, a performance problem with no off-the-shelf answer, or a new manufacturing process.</p><ul><li>US: federal R&amp;D credit under IRC §41, plus many state credits</li><li>Canada: SR&amp;ED, with refundable credits available to qualifying CCPCs</li></ul><blockquote>The credit isn't for what you invented. It's for the uncertainty you resolved getting there.</blockquote><h4>Documentation is the whole game</h4><p>Claims succeed or fail on contemporaneous documentation: project descriptions, time tracking and a clear statement of the uncertainty.</p><div class="callout"><b>Start now:</b> a lightweight monthly log of qualifying projects and hours dramatically strengthens a claim.</div>`},
{id:"blog-3",cat:"Startup Finance",date:"Jul 28, 2026",read:8,title:"ASC 606 for SaaS: Deferred Revenue Without the Headache",sub:"Simple rules for complex billing models",tags:["ASC 606","SaaS","Deferred revenue"],body:`<p>ASC 606 is conceptually simple (recognise revenue as you satisfy performance obligations) until you have annual contracts, upgrades, ramp deals, credits and multi-element arrangements.</p><h4>The five-step model, applied to SaaS</h4><p>Identify the contract and its performance obligations, determine the price, allocate it, and recognise revenue as each obligation is satisfied. For an annual subscription, that means 1/12 each month, not the cash when it lands.</p><ul><li>Annual prepaid subscription: recognise monthly over the term</li><li>Setup fees: distinct obligation or spread over the term?</li><li>Ramp deals: follow the pattern of value delivered</li><li>Usage-based components: recognise as usage occurs</li></ul><blockquote>Cash tells you about liquidity. Revenue tells you about performance.</blockquote><h4>Mid-term upgrades</h4><p>An upgrade may be a separate contract, a termination-and-new-contract, or a prospective modification. Each produces a different schedule, and auditors will check.</p><div class="callout"><b>Build the schedule once, correctly:</b> a clean deferred-revenue waterfall per contract is worth more at fundraising than any retrospective cleanup.</div>`},
{id:"blog-8",cat:"Startup Finance",date:"Aug 22, 2026",read:6,title:"Burn Rate & Runway: The Two Numbers Every Founder Must Know",sub:"Reading the fuel gauge before you run out",tags:["Burn rate","Runway","Fundraising"],body:`<p>Burn rate (how fast you spend) and runway (how long the cash lasts) are the two most important numbers on a founder's dashboard.</p><h4>Gross vs. net burn</h4><p>Gross burn is total monthly cash out. Net burn is cash out minus cash in, and it's the number investors care about.</p><div class="callout"><b>Runway = cash on hand ÷ net monthly burn.</b> With US$1.2M and US$100K net burn you have twelve months, but plan to raise with at least six left.</div><h4>The burn multiple</h4><p>Net burn divided by net new ARR. Under 1x is excellent; above 2x means growth is expensive.</p><blockquote>Founders who raise with two months left raise on someone else's terms.</blockquote><h4>Managing runway proactively</h4><p>Model base, downside and upside cases and revisit them monthly.</p>`},
{id:"blog-9",cat:"Startup Finance",date:"Jul 15, 2026",read:7,title:"409A Valuations Explained: What Founders Need to Know",sub:"Setting a defensible strike price",tags:["409A","Stock options","Valuation"],body:`<p>A 409A valuation sets the fair market value of common stock, which becomes the strike price for employee options. Get it wrong and employees face tax penalties under IRC §409A.</p><h4>Why it exists</h4><p>An independent, qualified valuation gives you a safe harbour: the IRS presumes your strike price is reasonable unless it can prove otherwise.</p><ul><li>Required before your first option grants</li><li>Refresh at least every 12 months</li><li>Refresh after any material event, such as a priced round</li></ul><blockquote>A stale 409A is a ticking liability.</blockquote><h4>Common approaches</h4><p>Backsolve from the latest preferred round, option-pricing models to allocate value across share classes, or market and income approaches for later-stage companies.</p>`},
{id:"blog-10",cat:"Payroll",date:"Aug 25, 2026",read:6,title:"Multi-State Payroll: Where Remote Teams Create Tax Traps",sub:"The compliance cost of a distributed workforce",tags:["Payroll","Multi-state","Withholding"],body:`<p>Remote work turned payroll into a multi-jurisdiction exercise. One employee working from another state can create withholding, unemployment-insurance and even nexus obligations.</p><h4>Where you withhold</h4><p>Generally you withhold for the state where work is physically performed. Reciprocity agreements can simplify this, but they're the exception.</p><ul><li>Register for withholding in each work state</li><li>Watch for local taxes (Ohio, Pennsylvania and others)</li><li>Set up state unemployment accounts per work state</li><li>Track "convenience of the employer" rules</li></ul><blockquote>One remote hire can create three registration obligations before their first payslip.</blockquote><div class="callout"><b>Get ahead of it:</b> keep a live map of where every employee works and register before the first payroll.</div>`},
{id:"blog-11",cat:"Payroll",date:"Aug 08, 2026",read:5,title:"Canadian Payroll Year-End: T4s Without the Scramble",sub:"Source deductions, slips and reconciliations",tags:["Canada payroll","CRA","T4"],body:`<p>Canadian payroll year-end concentrates a year of source deductions into a few weeks of slips, summaries and reconciliations. Small errors through the year become big ones in February.</p><h4>Reconcile as you go</h4><p>Reconcile CPP, EI and income-tax remittances to payroll registers every period, not just at year-end.</p><ul><li>Tie remittances to the payroll register each period</li><li>Confirm taxable benefits are processed through payroll</li><li>Review employee data before slips are produced</li><li>Reconcile the PD7A balance to the general ledger</li></ul><div class="callout"><b>Prepare early:</b> a quarterly mini year-end makes the real one routine.</div>`},
{id:"blog-12",cat:"Industry",date:"Aug 14, 2026",read:7,title:"E-Commerce Accounting: Why Your P&L Lies About Amazon",sub:"Untangling fees, reserves and settlement reports",tags:["E-commerce","Amazon","Shopify","A2X"],body:`<p>E-commerce sellers routinely misread profitability because the payout from Amazon or Shopify Payments is a netted figure: sales minus fees, refunds, reserves and advertising. Book that deposit as revenue and your P&amp;L is fiction.</p><h4>Gross up, then break out</h4><p>Record gross sales as revenue, then record marketplace fees, payment fees, refunds and advertising as separate lines.</p><ul><li>Record gross sales, not net payouts</li><li>Break out marketplace, fulfilment and payment fees</li><li>Account for reserves and held funds</li><li>Match settlement periods to accounting periods</li></ul><blockquote>The deposit is not your revenue.</blockquote><h4>Tooling that helps</h4><p>A2X and Link My Books parse settlement reports and post itemised journals into QuickBooks or Xero.</p><div class="callout"><b>Channel-level margin is the goal:</b> once you gross up correctly, you can see which channels actually make money.</div>`},
{id:"blog-13",cat:"Industry",date:"Jul 25, 2026",read:8,title:"Real Estate Accounting: Rent Rolls, CAM and the Metrics Lenders Want",sub:"Built around how property managers work",tags:["Real estate","Rent roll","CAM","NOI"],body:`<p>Real estate accounting follows the property cycle: rent collection, CAM reconciliation and lender reporting.</p><h4>The rent roll is the source of truth</h4><p>A clean rent roll ties every unit to its tenant, lease terms, rent and balance. Reconcile it to the general ledger every month.</p><h4>CAM reconciliation</h4><p>CAM reconciliation trues up estimated billings against actual operating costs. Errors either shortchange the owner or trigger tenant disputes.</p><ul><li>Property- and portfolio-level financials</li><li>NOI reporting for owners and lenders</li><li>DSCR packages for financing</li><li>Trust accounting for tenant deposits</li></ul><blockquote>Lenders read your NOI and DSCR. Report in the language they finance in.</blockquote>`},
{id:"blog-14",cat:"Industry",date:"Aug 03, 2026",read:6,title:"Restaurant Accounting: Prime Cost Is the Number That Matters",sub:"Daily sales, tips and margins",tags:["Restaurant","Prime cost","POS"],body:`<p>Restaurants run on thin margins, and the most important metric is prime cost: cost of goods sold plus labour. When it drifts above roughly 60–65% of sales, profit evaporates.</p><h4>Why daily matters</h4><p>Reviewing numbers monthly means flying blind for weeks. Daily POS reconciliation and weekly prime-cost tracking let operators course-correct quickly.</p><ul><li>Daily POS-to-deposit reconciliation</li><li>Tip pooling and payroll integration</li><li>Food and beverage cost vs. theoretical</li><li>Per-location P&amp;Ls</li></ul><blockquote>Closing monthly means finding out about losses three weeks too late.</blockquote>`},
{id:"blog-15",cat:"Industry",date:"Jul 18, 2026",read:8,title:"Construction Accounting: WIP Schedules and Percentage of Completion",sub:"Project accounting for long jobs",tags:["Construction","WIP","Job costing"],body:`<p>In construction, revenue is earned over months and billing rarely matches work. The WIP schedule is the document sureties and lenders scrutinise most.</p><h4>Percentage of completion</h4><p>Revenue is recognised in proportion to costs incurred against total estimated costs. Overbill and you've borrowed against future work; underbill and you're financing the client.</p><ul><li>Job costing with honest cost-to-complete estimates</li><li>Over/under billings per job</li><li>Retention accounting</li><li>Change orders flowing to revenue</li></ul><blockquote>Over-billings are not profit; they're a loan from your future self.</blockquote>`},
{id:"blog-16",cat:"Advisory",date:"Aug 27, 2026",read:7,title:"The 13-Week Cash Flow Forecast",sub:"Short-horizon visibility when it matters most",tags:["Cash flow","Forecasting","Liquidity"],body:`<p>The 13-week cash forecast trades the long-range scope of an annual budget for near-term precision on when cash actually moves.</p><h4>Why 13 weeks</h4><p>One quarter is long enough to plan around and short enough to forecast accurately. You forecast actual receipts and disbursements.</p><ul><li>Start from confirmed receivables and realistic collection dates</li><li>Layer in payroll, rent, tax and debt service on due dates</li><li>Roll forward weekly and compare to actuals</li><li>Flag the lowest-balance week</li></ul><blockquote>A budget tells you if the year works. A 13-week forecast tells you if you make payroll on the 15th.</blockquote><div class="callout"><b>Discipline beats sophistication:</b> a simple model updated every Monday beats an elaborate one built once.</div>`},
{id:"blog-17",cat:"Advisory",date:"Aug 10, 2026",read:6,title:"KPIs That Actually Drive Decisions",sub:"Building a dashboard leadership will use",tags:["KPIs","Dashboards","Management reporting"],body:`<p>A good KPI has an owner, a target and a direct line to an action. Everything else is decoration.</p><h4>The test for a real KPI</h4><p>If this number moved 20% next month, would anyone act differently? If not, it doesn't belong on the dashboard.</p><ul><li>Gross margin by product or segment</li><li>Cash conversion cycle</li><li>Customer acquisition cost payback</li><li>Net revenue retention</li></ul><blockquote>If a metric can't be tied to a decision, it's a story, not a tool.</blockquote>`},
{id:"blog-18",cat:"Advisory",date:"Jul 20, 2026",read:7,title:"Preparing for Due Diligence: What Acquirers Examine",sub:"Getting your financial house in order",tags:["Due diligence","M&A","Quality of earnings"],body:`<p>Financial due diligence is where deals are re-priced, delayed or killed. Sellers who prepare early command better terms.</p><h4>Quality of earnings</h4><p>Buyers separate sustainable profit from one-off items. Unsupported add-backs get stripped out of the multiple.</p><ul><li>Clean accrual financials for at least three years</li><li>A supportable normalised EBITDA</li><li>Revenue recognition that holds up</li><li>Reconciled working capital and net debt</li></ul><blockquote>Every dollar of EBITDA you can't defend costs you the multiple.</blockquote><div class="callout"><b>Start a year out:</b> the cheapest time to fix your books is before a buyer reads them.</div>`},
{id:"blog-19",cat:"Compliance",date:"Aug 16, 2026",read:6,title:"Audit-Ready All Year: Building Workpapers Reviewers Trust",sub:"The prepare-side discipline that makes audits painless",tags:["Audit","Workpapers","PBC"],body:`<p>Audits are only painful when preparation is left to the last minute. Reconciled accounts and organised support all year turn the audit into a confirmation exercise.</p><h4>What reviewer-ready means</h4><p>Every material balance has a schedule that ties to the ledger, cross-references source documents and explains any judgement.</p><ul><li>Lead schedules tied to the trial balance</li><li>Clear cross-referencing</li><li>Tie-outs and roll-forwards</li><li>Documented estimates and rationale</li></ul><blockquote>The best compliment an auditor can pay your workpapers is silence.</blockquote>`},
{id:"blog-20",cat:"Compliance",date:"Jul 12, 2026",read:7,title:"Trust & IOLTA Accounting: No Margin for Error",sub:"Why law-firm bookkeeping is different",tags:["IOLTA","Trust accounting","Law firms"],body:`<p>Client trust accounts hold money that isn't the firm's, and the standard for handling it is near-absolute. One commingled dollar can trigger a bar complaint.</p><h4>The cardinal rules</h4><p>Client funds are segregated and never used for firm expenses. Every client ledger is reconciled, and their sum must equal the trust bank balance.</p><ul><li>Three-way reconciliation: bank, book and client ledgers</li><li>No client balance ever goes negative</li><li>Earned fees transferred promptly and documented</li><li>Bank fees never drawn from trust principal</li></ul><blockquote>In trust accounting, "close enough" doesn't exist.</blockquote>`}
];

